{"statement_verdicts":[],"assumption_verdicts":[{"obj_id":"ass:domains-compact-borel","verdict":"faithful-refinement","note":"Matches the paper's assumption. Compactness: IsCompact Yset ∧ CompactSpace X ∧ CompactSpace Z. Borel σ-fields: the ambient section variables [BorelSpace X] [BorelSpace Z] (Basic.lean:24) tie each measurable structure to the given topology; Yset ⊆ ℝ uses the Borel σ-field of ℝ. Standard Borel: StandardBorelSpace X ∧ StandardBorelSpace Z (also ambient). The other domains are compact by construction: U ∈ [0,1] (rangeU) and W ∈ {0,1} (rangeW). The extra Yset.Nonempty is what the paper's endpoints y_min/y_max (min and max of the outcome domain) already require, and it also follows from PotentialSupport under a probability law. Neither direction fails."},{"obj_id":"ass:outcome-endpoints-known","verdict":"faithful-refinement","note":"'Known' is an epistemic remark, so its formal content is that y_min and y_max are the feasible endpoints of the outcome domain 𝒴. IsLeast Yset yMin ∧ IsGreatest Yset yMax says exactly that: the endpoints are the min and max of 𝒴, both attained, which compactness and nonemptiness already guarantee. yMin and yMax are fixed model fields (deterministic, not estimated), which matches 'treated as known'. Both directions hold."},{"obj_id":"ass:structure-potential-support","verdict":"faithful-refinement","note":"Y(w) ∈ 𝒴 a.s. for w ∈ {0,1} is realized as (∀ᵐ s, Y0 s ∈ Yset) ∧ (∀ᵐ s, Y1 s ∈ Yset) under the structural law M.law. This is the same statement, with one a.s. conjunct for each treatment arm."},{"obj_id":"ass:structure-consistency","verdict":"faithful-refinement","note":"Lean states ∀ᵐ s ∂law, Y s = W s * Y1 s + (1 - W s) * Y0 s under the structural law. This is exactly binding pin ass:pin-12 ('almost surely under the structural population law'), with W ∈ {0,1} from rangeW and real-valued potential outcomes. Both directions hold."},{"obj_id":"ass:structure-instrument-exogeneity","verdict":"faithful-refinement","note":"Z ⫫ (U,Y(0),Y(1)) | X is stated as: the conditional law of (Z,(U,Y0,Y1)) given X equals instKernel x ⊗ condDistrib((U,Y0,Y1)|X)(x) for P_X-a.e. x. Here instKernel =ᵐ condDistrib Zv Xv (field instKernel_version). The spaces involved are standard Borel (Z, and ℝ³) and the maps are measurable (measZ, measU, measY0, measY1, measX). On such spaces, conditional independence given X is equivalent to the conditional joint law factorizing a.e. into the product of the conditional marginals (rectangles form a countably generated π-system). So the statement is equivalent in both directions, with no Lean-only restriction."}],"substrate_gates":[],"escalate":null}

