{"statement_verdicts":[],"assumption_verdicts":[{"obj_id":"ass:target-weight-integrable","verdict":"faithful-refinement","note":"The Lean is Integrable (uncurry om) on xLaw M ⊗ Leb|[0,1], with xLaw = P_X. By Tonelli, the norm-integral equals E_X[∫_0^1 |ω(X,u)| du], so finiteness is the same in both directions. Icc versus (0,1) differs only on a Lebesgue-null set. The extra AEStronglyMeasurable component is the joint measurability that the paper's iterated expectation presupposes; consumers also carry it separately as TargetWeightMeasurable. So the Lean is equivalent to the paper's assumption."},{"obj_id":"ass:continuity-p-components","verdict":"faithful-refinement","note":"Finite (ConnectedComponents Z) says exactly that Z has finitely many connected components. The topology is the ambient [TopologicalSpace Z], which is the instrument domain's own topology; the core gives 𝒵 as a compact standard Borel domain, pinned by DomainsCompactBorel together with BorelSpace/StandardBorelSpace on the consumers. Equivalent in both directions."},{"obj_id":"ass:continuity-p-on-components","verdict":"faithful-refinement","note":"The Lean is ∀ᵐ x ∂P_X, ∀ z0, ContinuousOn (fun z => M.prop z x) (connectedComponent z0). Every connected component is connectedComponent z0 for some z0, and ContinuousOn on the component means continuity on it in the subspace topology. This is exactly 'continuous on each connected component' for P_X-a.e. x, the reading fixed by pin-16. M.prop is the model's p(z,x). Equivalent."},{"obj_id":"ass:phi-smooth-differentiable","verdict":"faithful-refinement","note":"The Lean is ∀ z x, ∀ p ∈ [0,1], HasDerivWithinAt (phi z x) (dphi z x p) (Icc 0 1) p. For p in (0,1), Icc is a neighbourhood of p, so this is ordinary differentiability. At p=0 it is the right derivative and at p=1 the left derivative, both finite. So it matches pin-17 exactly. dphi is pinned on [0,1] as the derivative because derivatives within Icc 0 1 are unique, so it is ∂φ/∂p with the endpoint values pin-17 assigns. Values of phi outside [0,1] play no role."},{"obj_id":"ass:phi-smooth-derivative","verdict":"faithful-refinement","note":"The Lean is ∃ C L ≥ 0 such that, for all z, x and all p, p' ∈ [0,1], |dphi z x p| ≤ C and |dphi z x p − dphi z x p'| ≤ L|p − p'|. This is word for word pin-18: a uniform bound and a uniform Lipschitz constant over z ∈ 𝒵 and x ∈ 𝒳. dphi is ∂φ/∂p on [0,1], as established by PolicyDifferentiable."}],"substrate_gates":[],"escalate":null}

