{"statement_verdicts":[],"assumption_verdicts":[{"obj_id":"ass:discrete-outcome-gap","verdict":"faithful-refinement","note":"Both directions hold. The Lean is ∃ ch>0, ∀ k<K with 1≤l_k, ∀ j≤l_k, ∀ᵐ x under the law of X (xLaw = law of M.Xv), ch ≤ |p_{k+1}(x) − J_full,k(x) − q_{j,k}(x)|. That is the paper's statement word for word: one c_h, the same k range, the l_k≥1 guard, j=0..l_k, and 'almost surely' read under the law of X. Jfull unfolds to (p_{k+1}−p_k)·∫y d(discreteGapLaw). discreteGapLaw is the normalized difference of the canonical treated submeasures at p_{k+1} and p_k, which is the identified complier law μ_{1,k|x}. This matches def:discrete-nuisance-components, J_full,k = (p_{k+1}−p_k)E_{μ_{1,k|x}}[Y]. LevelData is a free parameter that the consumers instantiate."},{"obj_id":"ass:1d-ot-probability-laws","verdict":"faithful-refinement","note":"mu and nu are Measure ℝ with IsProbabilityMeasure. Qmu and Qnu equal Causalean.Stat.quantile on (0,1), and that function is sInf{x | τ ≤ cdf μ x} (checked by hover and in Causalean/Stat/Quantile/Quantile.lean:55-63). For a probability measure cdf μ x = μ((−∞,x]), so this is exactly the binding lower generalized inverse (ass:pin-20, ass:pin-63, and the orchestrator's quantile ruling) on 0<t<1. Equivalent in both directions."},{"obj_id":"ass:1d-ot-moment-support","verdict":"faithful-refinement","note":"Bounded support of a measure on ℝ holds iff ∃B with mu([−B,B]ᶜ)=0. If supp ⊆ [−B,B], the complement of the support is null because ℝ is second countable. Conversely, an open null set misses the support. For a probability measure, Integrable id nu is a finite first moment (id is measurable). The disjunction covers 'or vice versa' exactly. Equivalent."},{"obj_id":"ass:1d-ot-product-cost","verdict":"faithful-refinement","note":"∀ a b, c a b = a*b is literally the cost c(a,b)=a·b, so the two are equivalent."},{"obj_id":"ass:sharpness-observed-model","verdict":"faithful-refinement","note":"The Lean asks for a structural model N whose observed law (Y,W,Z,X) equals obs and that satisfies StructuralAssumptions: potential support, consistency, exogeneity, threshold crossing, uniform resistance, common support, plus the setting's compact/Borel domains. That is the paper's 'P_obs generated by a true structural model satisfying asp:structure'. N ranges over any sample space in universe u. The two extra conjuncts restate the paper's fixed setting rather than restricting it. SameKnownWorld pins the same 𝒴, y_min, y_max, d_X and covariate coordinates as the ambient setting M; the paper treats 𝒴 and 𝒳 as fixed setting objects. OutcomeEndpointsKnown (y_min=min 𝒴, y_max=max 𝒴) is the paper's own definition of the endpoints (core setting). Neither narrows the paper's models, so the two are equivalent."}],"substrate_gates":[],"escalate":null}

