{"statement_verdicts":[],"assumption_verdicts":[{"obj_id":"ass:structure-threshold-crossing","verdict":"faithful-refinement","note":"Equivalent to the paper's assumption read under pin-13. Conjunct 1 is W = 1(U ≤ p(Z,X)) almost surely under the structural law. Conjunct 4 says that for zxLaw-a.e. (z,x), the mean of W's conditional distribution given (Z,X)=(z,x) equals prop z x; since rangeW makes W 0/1-valued, that mean is P(W=1 | Z=z, X=x). So the field prop is a version of p(Z,X) = P(W=1|Z,X). Joint measurability of prop and the [0,1] range are properties every version of a conditional probability has, by Doob–Dynkin, so they add nothing. The range conjunct also repeats the structure field rangeProp. Lean ⇒ paper: immediate. Paper ⇒ Lean: take p to be a measurable version. Changing p on a zxLaw-null set changes p(Z,X) only on a null set of Ω, so the a.s. threshold equation still holds."},{"obj_id":"ass:structure-uniform-resistance","verdict":"faithful-refinement","note":"This is exactly pin-14: for xLaw-a.e. x, condDistrib U Xv law x = Causalean.Stat.unifOI. I checked unifOI in Causalean/Stat/Coupling/Monotone/ProductLoss/PIT.lean:45; it is volume.restrict (Ioo 0 1), the Unif(0,1) law. condDistrib is the conditional law of U given X, unique up to an xLaw-null set, so the a.e. statement does not depend on which version is used. Both directions hold."},{"obj_id":"ass:structure-common-support","verdict":"faithful-refinement","note":"Supp(Z|X=x) = 𝒵 holds exactly when every nonempty open subset of 𝒵 has positive conditional mass (the support is the set of points all of whose open neighbourhoods have positive mass). The conditional law is defined only up to xLaw-null sets. By the up-to-null-sets convention, 'for all x' means some Markov version has the property at every x, and that is the Lean's ∃ κ, Markov, κ =ᵐ condDistrib Zv Xv, ∀ x, ∀ nonempty open A, 0 < κ x A. The section variables give [TopologicalSpace Z] [BorelSpace Z] (Basic.lean:22-24), so open sets are measurable and the topology is the one of 𝒵. Under the owner's ruling, finite 𝒵 carries the discrete topology, so this means every instrument value has positive conditional probability. Both directions hold."},{"obj_id":"ass:target-weight-measurable","verdict":"faithful-refinement","note":"The Lean is joint measurability of (x,u) ↦ ω(x,u) on X × Icc 0 1, using the product σ-algebra with the Borel subtype structure on [0,1]. That is exactly 'ω: 𝒳×[0,1]→ℝ is jointly measurable'. Values of om off [0,1] are not constrained, which matches the paper's domain. The two are equivalent."},{"obj_id":"ass:target-weight-identifiable","verdict":"faithful-refinement","note":"This is pin-15. Take any two structural models, on any sample spaces in a fixed universe, that satisfy StructuralAssumptions and have the same observedLaw (the joint law of (Y,W,Z,X)). Their ω rules then agree a.e. under xLaw ⊗ Leb restricted to [0,1]. xLaw is the X-marginal of P_obs, so this is the paper's P_obs(dx) du-a.e. agreement. The extra premises SameKnownWorld (the same 𝒴, y_min, y_max, d_X and covariate coordinates) and OutcomeEndpointsKnown only restrict the comparison to the paper's fixed known setting: core node ass:outcome-endpoints-known says the outcome domain and its endpoints are treated as known, and def:outcome-endpoints defines the endpoints. Within the paper's model class nothing is dropped and nothing is added, so the two are equivalent."}],"substrate_gates":[],"escalate":null}

