{"statement_verdicts":[],"assumption_verdicts":[{"obj_id":"ass:ot-formulation-treated-compatibility","verdict":"faithful-refinement","note":"Both directions hold. The Lean says: for obs.zx-a.e. (z,x) and every measurable A, the lintegral over u in Ioc 0 p(z,x) of condKernel(pi1)(u,x)(A) equals H_{1,x}(p(z,x),A). That is equality of measures applied to every measurable set, which is the paper's identity of measures holding P_obs-a.e. Ioc vs [0,p] differs only by a Lebesgue-null set. The consumer instantiates obs as transportData M, so zx is zxLaw M (its x-marginal is xLaw M), H is canonSubmeasure, and prop is M.prop, which lies in [0,1] by rangeProp. Under pin-29 the constraints are imposed jointly with FeasibleSupport (pi1 is a probability measure supported on u in [0,1]) and UniformCovariateMarginal (pi1.fst = Leb(0,1) ⊗ xLaw). Hence the Dirac fallback branch of outcomeKernel is never used, and condKernel is a disintegration version that is unique for xLaw-a.e. x and Lebesgue-a.e. u in (0,1). So the u-integral, and therefore the a.e.-(z,x) identity, does not depend on which version of π̃_1(dy|u,x) is chosen, as the null-set convention requires."},{"obj_id":"ass:ot-formulation-untreated-compatibility","verdict":"faithful-refinement","note":"Mirror of the treated case: the integral over u in Ioc p(z,x) 1 of condKernel(pi0)(u,x)(A) equals H_{0,x}(p,A) for every measurable A, obs.zx-a.e. (z,x). This is the paper's measure identity, and the endpoint differences are Lebesgue-null. Under the joint constraints (pin-29: FeasibleSupport, UniformCovariateMarginal) and obs = transportData M (zx marginal consistent with xLaw, p in [0,1]), the identity does not depend on the choice of disintegration version, and the non-finite fallback branch of outcomeKernel is unreachable."},{"obj_id":"ass:ot-formulation-uniform-covariate-marginal","verdict":"faithful-refinement","note":"pi_w.fst is the (u,x)-marginal of a measure on (ℝ×X)×ℝ. unifOI is volume restricted to Ioo 0 1 (Causalean P