{"statement_verdicts":[{"obj_id":"def:dml-continuous-outcome-algorithm","verdict":"drift","note":"The Lean definition is strictly narrower than the algorithm. In DMLLearningSteps the fits Jplus and Jminus are forced through clipBand to [min 0 yMin, max 0 yMax], and JfullPlus and JfullMinus through clipOutcome to [yMin, yMax]. ContinuousFitScope then adds ScoreNuisancesClipped (ContClipping, the realization of ass:dml-normality-score-clipping and pin-23), which requires those same ranges. The frozen algorithm only says to 'Estimate conditional expectations M^±, J^±, J_full^± by flexible ML'. It clips only the conditional probabilities (to [0,1], and π̂, τ̂ further to [c_π/2,1]) and ν̂ (to [y_min,y_max]). Clipping the J fits is a separate assumption of thm:dml-normality (ass:dml-normality-score-clipping), not a step of the algorithm. So an algorithm run whose J learner returns values outside these ranges is not an IsDMLContinuousAlgorithm instance: paper ⟹ Lean fails. FIX: in DMLLearningSteps set e.Jplus/e.Jminus/e.JfullPlus/e.JfullMinus to the raw `fit` output, with no clipBand or clipOutcome. Replace `ScoreNuisancesClipped` in ContinuousFitScope by the clipping the algorithm itself does: prop/pLev/higherPolicyFit/higherBaselineFit/gamma/gammaTail/M± in [0,1], piLev/tauLev in [cpi/2,1], gammaFull = difference of the two clipped fits, simplex shares, nu in [yMin,yMax]. Leave the J ranges to ass:dml-normality-score-clipping as a hypothesis of the theorem. The other parts check out against the frozen node. The first stage is clipped and fit on the I₁ rows, with q̂ = φ(z,x,p̂). p̄₂ and q̄₂ are averages over I₂. Ŝ₀ = {z : p̄₂(z) ≤ ρ_n}, with ρ_n positive, decreasing, tending to 0 and n^{1/4}ρ_n → ∞ (pin-4); the baseline vertices of Ŝ₀ are removed, edges are |Δ| < gap/2 (pin-3), and the components give the ordered sets. p̂_k is fit on Z∈Ŝ_k with response W, and p̂_0 is absent. γ_full is the difference of two clipped fits. π̂ and τ̂ use clipDenom, and the shares are simplex-valued. ν̂ comes from the moment regression and is clipped. Minus-side objects are omitted at k=0 (by index type). θ̂ is the I₂ average of ψ̂ and σ̂² is |I₂|⁻¹Σ(ψ̂−θ̂)². EqualHalfSplit realizes the split under binding ruling (3). The measurability clauses only record that the fits depend on the I₁ data and the recovered labels."},{"obj_id":"def:observations-and-nuisances","verdict":"matched","note":"ObservedSample = Causalean.Stat.IIDSample A (Obs X Z) μ (observationLaw M). Its fields are: measurable draws Z i; iIndepFun; identically distributed with Z 0; and law of Z 0 = observationLaw. observationLaw is the pushforward of the structural law under s ↦ (X, Z, W, Y), and Obs = X × Z × ℝ × ℝ in the order (X, Z, W, Y). Together this is exactly 'an i.i.d. sample O_i of O=(X,Z,W,Y) ~ P_obs'. Indexing the draws over ℕ is the usual encoding of an n-sample. The clause 'η collects the nuisance functions, η̂ its estimate' is notation, realized by the NuisanceCont carrier. It adds no constraint on the sample."},{"obj_id":"def:covariate-continuous-lower-bound-rank-kernel","verdict":"matched","note":"Both implications hold on the gap domain. weightCDF is weightLaw(Iic v), i.e. F_{ω,k|x}(v), and weightCDFLeft is weightLaw(Iio v), i.e. F_{ω,k|x}(v−). randomizedRank = F(ω(x,u)−) + s[F(ω(x,u)) − F(ω(x,u)−)], which is R_{k,x}(u,s). gapAttainingLaw x k u = unifOI.map (s ↦ Q x k (1 − R(u,s))), so its mass on A is ∫₀¹ 1{Q_{Y,k|x}(1 − R_{k,x}(u,s)) ∈ A} ds, i.e. eq:pf_cts_rank_kernel. IsAttainingKernelVersion forces K(u,x) = gapAttainingLaw x k u for every k < count and every u ∈ G_k(x), for P_X-a.e. x. That is the paper's formula under the null-set convention for the conditional objects (μ_{1,k|x}, the conditional gaps; pins 14, 16, 32). The Q parameter matches pin-48. RankKernelExtensionReading makes Q jointly measurable and 𝒴-valued on [0,1], and equal to the lower generalized quantile of gapLaw (μ_{1,k|x}) on (0,1), as in pin-46/59 and ruling (1). bk-3's endpoint pins y_min/y_max are a theorem condition and one admissible such extension. The Classical.choose witness comes from exists_attaining_kernel_version under the theorem's own hypotheses. Those are StructuralAssumptions, OutcomeEndpointsKnown, TargetWeightMeasurable, PropensityContinuousOnComponents and FiniteInstrumentComponents; the last is the @node ass:continuity-p-components. The witness's restriction to the gaps is the NL object for a.e. x, whichever version is chosen. Off the gaps, the kernel carries the pasted law (tail Dirac, treated-outcome kernel). That is a Lean-only extension outside this node's domain, and it is judged at the consuming theorem. Supportedness on 𝒴 and the Markov property follow from Q ∈ 𝒴 on [0,1]. The 'randomized probability integral transform' sentence is a supporting fact, not a defining clause, and is left to the proof."}],"assumption_verdicts":[],"substrate_gates":[],"escalate":{"kind":"scaffold-mismatch","obj_id":"def:dml-continuous-outcome-algorithm","reason":"IsDMLContinuousAlgorithm builds the separate thm:dml-normality assumption ass:dml-normality-score-clipping into the algorithm. J^±_{j,k} and J^±_{full,k} are forced through clipBand/clipOutcome in DMLLearningSteps, and ScoreNuisancesClipped (ContClipping) is required in ContinuousFitScope. The frozen algorithm estimates M^±, J^±, J_full^± 'by flexible ML' and clips only the conditional probabilities, π̂/τ̂ and ν̂. Remove the J clipping and the J-range conjuncts from the definition and keep them as the theorem's own hypothesis.","unpinned":"","witness":null}}

