{"statement_verdicts":[],"assumption_verdicts":[{"obj_id":"ass:dml-equal-split","verdict":"faithful-refinement","note":"Unfolded, with S.Z i the i-th whole observation (Causalean IIDSample.Z). For every even n≥2 the Lean requires: I₁ n and I₂ n are almost surely disjoint, their union is range n, and each has n/2 elements; both are measurable; I₁ n is uniform over the n/2-subsets of range n (μ{I₁=J}=1/C(n,n/2)); and I₁ n is independent of (O_0,…,O_{n-1}). I₂ n is almost surely the complement of I₁ n, so it is independent as well. This is exactly the binding ruling (3): a uniform partition into two halves drawn independently of the observations. Restricting to even n only spells out what 'each of size n/2' presupposes. Measurability is bookkeeping implied by 'random'. Both directions hold."},{"obj_id":"ass:dml-simplex-shares","verdict":"faithful-refinement","note":"Both branches (Cont and Bin) unfold to: for every RegularIndex a=(k,j) with k≤K and 1≤j≤l_k, and every x, the share is ≥0 for all z and sums to 1 over L.T k j. The paper says 'for every nonempty T̂_{j,k}'. By the level-set construction in def:policy-level-sets, every T_{j,k} with 1≤j≤l_k is the set where q attains its j-th value, so it is nonempty; empty sets occur only at the boundary indices j=0 and j=l_k+1, which RegularIndex excludes. Quantifying over all regular indices therefore matches the paper's 'nonempty' quantification for every paper model. Requiring nonnegativity for all z ∈ Z, not only z ∈ T̂, is a conservative extension: the multinomial learner's output extends by 0 outside T̂, which matches the population convention a=0 outside T. The development also restricts shares to T before use (restrictContShares), so values outside T̂ never affect a consumer. Both directions hold."},{"obj_id":"ass:bk-2","verdict":"faithful-refinement","note":"LevelCountPos' K := LevelCountPos K := 1 ≤ K, which is exactly 'K ≥ 1'. The MeasurableSpace instance after it in the extraction is unrelated context."},{"obj_id":"ass:pin-65","verdict":"faithful-refinement","note":"error n a c = eLpNorm (fit n a c − truth c) 2 (xLaw M). The fitted function is held fixed (n, a, c fixed) and integration is over the population law of X. xLaw is the law of X under the structural law, which is the X-marginal of the observation law that pushes it forward. Squaring eLpNorm at p=2 gives E_X|f̂−f|², so this is exactly the pinned population L2 norm."},{"obj_id":"ass:pin-22","verdict":"faithful-refinement","note":"FirstStageRate unfolds to ExtendedLittleOp μ (n ↦ max_z l2Error M (pHat (2(n+1)) a z) (p z)) ((2(n+1))^{-1/4}). That is, for every ε>0, μ{ε·N^{-1/4} ≤ max_z ‖p̂(z,·)−p(z,·)‖_{L2(P_X)}} → 0 along N=2(n+1), which is N^{1/4}·max_z‖·‖ →P 0. l2Error evaluates the norm with the fitted function fixed. This equals the conditional expectation given I₁ at a fresh, independent X with the population marginal law, as the first clause of the pin states. Even sample sizes only are the domain the equal split presupposes (halves of size n/2). Both directions hold."}],"substrate_gates":[],"escalate":null}

