{"statement_verdicts":[{"obj_id":"thm:1d-ot","verdict":"matched","note":"I unfolded every definition the statement uses. (1) IsQuantilePair: mu and nu are probability measures, and on Ioo 0 1 Qmu and Qnu equal Causalean.Stat.quantile, which is sInf {x | t ≤ cdf rho x}. For a probability measure that is inf{a : rho((-inf,a]) ≥ t}, exactly pins ass:pin-63 and ass:pin-20 and the orchestrator's ruling (non-strict lower inverse on (0,1)). Q outside (0,1) is unconstrained, and no statement uses it there, since 1-t stays in (0,1). (2) BoundedSupportOrFirstMoment is the paper's disjunction: '∃B, mu([-B,B]ᶜ)=0' is bounded support (ℝ is second countable, so the support lies in [-B,B] iff that mass is 0), and 'Integrable id nu' is a finite first moment; the symmetric case is the second disjunct. (3) IsProductCost is c a b = a*b. (4) IsCoupling is the Causalean structure: a probability measure whose map Prod.fst is mu and whose map Prod.snd is nu, i.e. Π(mu,nu). Conclusion: IsLeast and IsGreatest over the set of coupling values {∫ c dπ} give the min and max and their existence. ∫ over Ioo 0 1 is ∫_0^1. The two conjuncts unifOI.map (Qmu t, Qnu(1-t)) and unifOI.map (Qmu t, Qnu t) say these are couplings, and the two integral equalities say they attain the min and max (unifOI = volume restricted to (0,1), the law of ξ). The extra first conjunct, that the cost is integrable under every coupling, makes the Bochner integrals meaningful rather than defaulting to 0. It is a proved strengthening that the paper's hypotheses imply. No hypothesis was added. Lean and paper are equivalent in both directions."}],"assumption_verdicts":[],"substrate_gates":[{"name":"Papers.TbsPrteOptimalTransport.productCost_transport_extrema","statement":"For probability laws mu, nu on ℝ with lower quantiles Qmu, Qnu on (0,1), where one law has bounded support and the other a finite first moment, and with cost c(a,b)=ab: the cost is integrable under every coupling; the minimum over Π(mu,nu) is ∫_0^1 Qmu(t)Qnu(1-t)dt, attained by the countermonotone coupling; the maximum is ∫_0^1 Qmu(t)Qnu(t)dt, attained by the comonotone coupling.","classical_fact":"1D optimal transport with a supermodular/product cost: the comonotone (quantile) coupling is optimal (Villani, Topics in Optimal Transportation, 1D case)","missing_infra":"","gate_class":"cited","source":{"cite_id":"cite:villani2021topics","locator":"1D optimal transport with product cost (no numbered locator given in the paper)"},"check_status":"cited-verified-attested"}],"escalate":null}

